text:
anya.design: Dropping a quick thread on the next dark-theme pass. Main issues: inline code legibility in doc panes and overlapping chart lines on small screens. Figma link: https://www.figma.com/file/efgh5678/Dark-Theme-Refresh — pls react.
zoe.design: Saw it. The code tokens read okay at 16px but inline snippets at 12px feel faint. Might need to shift foreground or add tight background pill. :point_up_2:
rachel.eng: For the doc panes, adding background pill is easiest for us. Canvas charts are trickier. What are current tokens?
anya.design: Current token dump: ```css
--bg-deep: #050708;
--panel: #0b0f11;
--text-dim: #95a0a6;
--text-normal: #e6eef0;
--code-bg: #061214;
--code-fg: #bfe6ff;
--hl-keyword: #6be3ff;
--hl-string: #ffd9b2;
--accent-1: #2ac6b0;
--accent-2: #ff6d5f;
--accent-3: #f4b400;
```
tom.frontend: Tried rendering the chart with those accents plus 0.6 alpha for area fills. On small canvases the coral and amber collapse into the background because our compositing uses 'multiply' for perf. We can avoid extra draw calls if colors are slightly brighter (no halo).
keiko.a11y: Ran quick contrast checks: --text-dim on --panel = 3.1:1 (fails). --code-fg on --code-bg = 4.8:1 (ok for large code, marginal for inline).
luis.data: I ran a colorblindness sim. Accent-1 vs Accent-2 has low separation in deuteranopia when saturated down. Suggest shifting accent-1 to #00b39f and accent-2 to #ff5a50. Also for charts keep one hue pair with different lightness rather than multiple close chroma hues.
style-bot: Auto lint: recommend --text-dim -> #c3d0d6 to reach >=4.5:1 on --panel. \o/
zoe.design: Two options: (1) Bump text-dim to #c3d0d6 and brighten accents slightly (A: minimal CSS changes), or (2) keep text-dim but add inline code pill (#07161a) and 0.5px outline for inline snippets (would need custom rendering in markdown renderer). Which is less friction?
rachel.eng: Option 1 is far less friction. Option 2 adds renderer work.
anya.design: I'm leaning Option 1. I'll update tokens and prepare a diff. Quick question re: copy — should the theme preference in settings read 'Dark mode' or 'Dark theme'? UX research had mixed answers.
keiko.a11y: Use 'Dark mode' — users commonly look for "mode" in assistive tech docs. Also please add a screen-reader hint for the toggle: aria-describedby pointing to a short explanation about high contrast.
tom.frontend: For charts, I propose a fallback dashed line style for overlapping traces on narrow widths — an easy CSS/Canvas switch. Thoughts?
lu is.data: (typo) luis.data: Dashed fallback makes sense; but dashed vs solid might be lost in very dense graphs. Consider combining a dashed pattern with a slightly thicker stroke + 10% brightness bump.
zoe.design: Agree. Also small accessibility nit: focus ring on table rows currently uses same color as selected state. Let's use --focus-ring: #ffd86b as keiko suggested earlier.
style-bot: Generated token patch suggestion: "--text-dim: #c3d0d6", "--accent-1: #00b39f", "--accent-2: #ff5a50", "--code-bg: #07161a", "--focus-ring: #ffd86b".
anya.design: Nice. I'll update Figma and open a token PR with the patch + changelog entry: "Improve dark theme legibility and chart separability". I'll @tom.frontend for renderer follow-ups. If no objections by EOD I'll merge.
keiko.a11y: I'll add a short checklist to the PR for smoke testing: keyboard nav, contrast spots, inline code, chart legend readability, screen reader announcement for theme toggle.
lu is.data: (luis): I'll post the full CVD sim results once the tokens are live.
anya.design: Thanks team — quick sync. :sparkles:
